# Eliminator sound board -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Sega/Gremlin G-80 games carry a sound board of their own, driven from the CPU board's
# output ports: the port bits arrive here as ports (MAME's TTL_INPUT names, HI_D0 ...
# LO_D7 or SOUND_0 ...), and the mixed sound goes out to the amplifier.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/sega/nl_elim.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(elim). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Aaron Giles (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/sega/nl_elim.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 30: #define UNDERCLOCK_NOISE_GEN 1 -> 0 (a part run slower than the board runs it)
# line 31: #define HLE_BACKGROUND_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 32: #define HLE_TORPEDO1_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 33: #define HLE_TORPEDO2_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 34: #define HLE_SKITTER_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 35: #define ENABLE_FRONTIERS 1 -> 0 (solver partitioning only)
# line 43: #define ADD_CLIPPING_DIODES 1 -> 0 (diodes standing in for the chip's own input clamps)
# line 44: #define DISABLE_TORPEDO2_NOISE 1 -> 0 (a connection disabled for speed)
# line 1207: NET_C(GND, U17.1, U17.2, U17.5, U17.6) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
# line 1208: NET_C(GND, U26.1, U26.2, U26.4, U26.5, U26.12, U26.13) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
# line 1209: NET_C(GND, U27.13, U28.1) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
# line 1210: NET_C(GND, U31.11, U31.13) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
#
# Corrected from the sheet (the notes below say what was read where):
#   U5: the sheet's part is NE555, MAME's LM555 -- the sheet (800-3174 sheet 7) and parts list item 25 print NE555; MAME's LM555 is the same timer
#   U13: the sheet's part is NE555, MAME's LM555 -- the sheet (800-3174 sheet 7) and parts list item 25 print NE555; MAME's LM555 is the same timer
#   sheet 8: a CD4069 RC oscillator, which MAME replaces with an ideal clock: U32.6 U32.9 R142.1; U32.5 R142.2 C68.1; U32.8 C68.2 R137.1; U32.12 U32.11 R139.1; U32.13 R139.2 C65.1; U32.10 C65.2 R138.1; U32.2 U32.3 R143.1; U32.1 R143.2 C69.1; U32.4 C69.2 R144.2; U31.2 U31.3 R141.1; U31.1 R141.2 C67.1; U31.4 C67.2 R134.1
#   sheet 7: a CD4069 RC oscillator, which MAME replaces with an ideal clock: U31.6 U31.9 R140.1; U31.5 R140.2 C66.1; U31.8 C66.2 R135.1
# line 175: R_PSG_1 is not a part -- a 1K from +5V standing in for one output of the sound chip (PSG) MAME emulates separately; on the sheet U30's three outputs (pins 1, 4, 5) are tied straight onto R133
# line 176: R_PSG_2 is not a part -- a 1K from +5V standing in for one output of the sound chip (PSG) MAME emulates separately; on the sheet U30's three outputs (pins 1, 4, 5) are tied straight onto R133
# line 177: R_PSG_3 is not a part -- a 1K from +5V standing in for one output of the sound chip (PSG) MAME emulates separately; on the sheet U30's three outputs (pins 1, 4, 5) are tied straight onto R133
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D1), TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D2), TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D3), TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D4), TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D5), TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D6), TTL_INPUT(I_LO_D7), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D0), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D1), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D2), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D3), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D4), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D5), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D6), TTL_INPUT(I_HI_D7)
# lines 864-903: MAME's five ideal clocks (_200HZA, _200HZB, _10HZ, _50HZ, _100HZ), the
# oscillator parts it parks on GND in their place, and its HINT(NC)s on the inverter
# outputs, are dropped; the oscillators are put back from the sheet ("Corrected from the
# sheet", above).
#
# Left out by MAME (commented out at TAG, so not in this file either):
#   line 198: RES(R1, RES_K(100))     -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 199: RES(R2, RES_M(1))       -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 200: RES(R3, RES_K(22))      -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 201: RES(R4, RES_K(2.2))     -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 203: RES(R5, RES_K(10))      -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 216: RES(R11, RES_K(2.2))    -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 217: RES(R12, RES_M(1))      -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 218: RES(R13, RES_K(330))    -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 368: CAP(C1, CAP_U(4.7))     -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 369: CAP(C2, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 370: CAP(C3, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 371: CAP(C4, CAP_U(10))      -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 393: CAP(C22, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 394: CAP(C23, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 395: CAP(C24, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 396: CAP(C25, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 397: CAP(C26, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 398: CAP(C27, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 402: CAP(C31, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 403: CAP(C32, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 411: CAP(C40, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 412: CAP(C41, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 437: CAP(C62, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 445: CAP(C70, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 446: CAP(C71, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 447: CAP(C72, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 448: CAP(C73, CAP_U(10))
#   line 449: CAP(C74, CAP_U(0.1))
#   line 463: Q_2N4093(Q1)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 479: TL082_DIP(U2)           // Op. Amp. -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 588: AY_3_8912_DIP(U30)      // PSG -- emulated by MAME
#   line 598: TTL_74LS125_DIP(U33)    // Quad 3-state buffer
#   line 602: TTL_74LS374_DIP(U34)    // Octal D-Type Transparent Latches And Edge-Triggered Flip-Fl
#   line 606: TTL_74LS374_DIP(U35)    // Octal D-Type Transparent Latches And Edge-Triggered Flip-Fl
#   line 610: TTL_74LS74_DIP(U36)     // Dual D Flip Flop
#   line 614: TTL_74LS10_DIP(U37)     // Triple 3-Input NAND Gate
#   line 618: TTL_74LS14_DIP(U38)
#   line 622: TTL_74LS08_DIP(U39)     // Quad 2-Input AND Gates
#   line 626: TTL_74LS30_DIP(U40)     // 8-Input NAND Gate
#   line 630: TTL_74LS14_DIP(U41)
#
# Compared with the Sound Board drawing 800-3174 rev B, sheets 7 and 8 of 8 (doc
# cff008c00810 pp65-68, each sheet printed in two halves), and its parts list (pp30-35),
# 2026-10-20. Every passive value in this file agrees with the parts list -- 188 parts, none
# different -- and the list shows that R24 and C42, which MAME's file queries, do not exist.
# All values are the Eliminator ones in MAME's variant table (R79 2M is fitted), and no
# Zektor part or jumper is drawn. The latch bit assignment (U34 74LS374 for the HI port, U35
# for the LO port, D0 unused on the LO side), the 7406/7407 buffers, the noise source (U4,
# U3) and sum buffer U12, the thrust, skitter, enemy ship and background channels (U10, U11,
# U5, U1, U13, U15, U16, U25), the PSG input U29, explosions 1-3, fireball and bounce (U6-U9,
# U14, the CD4011 one-shots U17-U19), the torpedo VCOs and counters (U20-U23) and the summing
# resistors R6-R10 all agree with MAME pin for pin and value for value, transistor and diode
# orientation included; R36 does feed torpedo 2's D6, as with DISABLE_TORPEDO2_NOISE off.
#
# The 200 Hz A/B, 10 Hz, 50 Hz and 100 Hz clocks, which MAME runs as ideal clocks, are
# CD4069 RC oscillators on the sheet (U32 and U31 with R139-R143 and C65-C69), on exactly the
# pins MAME parks, with the values MAME's comment gives; they are put back here. U5 and U13
# are printed NE555 (MAME: LM555, the same part). The PSG's three outputs (U30 pins 1, 4, 5)
# are tied straight onto R133 680 ohm; R_PSG_1-3 are only MAME's model of the chip, so that
# node stays a port.
#
# Open questions, as printed and not settled: this scan drops two short line segments --
# Q3's base lead (sheet 7, zone C3) and U19 pin 4 into its second gate (sheet 8, zone D6) --
# where MAME's reading is the only one the drawing allows; the identical one-shots elsewhere
# on sheet 8 draw the line. MAME's is kept.
#
# Not in this file: the CPU interface (U30 AY-3-8912, U33-U41), the final amplifier and mute
# (U2, Q1, R1-R5, R11-R13, C1, C4) and the bypass capacitors, which are on the sheets with
# the values MAME's comments give. R3 is not part of the final amplifier's gain network, as
# MAME's comment says: it brings contact 3 onto the summing node.

